 
          <p>When live gives you lemons, make lemonade. Life never gives me lemons: I live in Belgium, not in Spain or Italy. Live gives me chores. Endless chores that keep on piling up the typical way they typically pile up in households with two small kids. There&rsquo;s multiple heaps of laundry loitering around (ha!) the house, a suspicious amount of missing or non-matching socks, shoes that never make it into the cabinet in the hallway, toys getting sucked up by the black hole also known as the dusty areas under the cupboard and sofa, forever-dirty windows thanks to grubby little fingers at a height of about ten to twenty centimetres, and dog hair&mdash;so much dog hair.</p>
<p>No lemon squeezing here but rather housekeeping, and a lot of it. Two minutes after I helped the youngest out of his chair this morning he was walking around with one sock. The black hole must have claimed the other as I couldn&rsquo;t find it and was in a rush to get to work. Sometimes the black hole is merciful and spits out something we&rsquo;ve been looking for. Sometimes it&rsquo;s not.</p>
<p>And yet I like the act of housekeeping, just as I like the act of cleaning up a messy piece of code to turn it into a neatly ordered, sense-making block that no longer requires a second look. Throwing clothes into the washing machine, the tumble dryer, folding and sorting them into piles, and finally putting them back where they belong in our respective bedrooms is a part of life that is severely underappreciated and perhaps also misunderstood.</p>
<p>Life is often mundane. Doing mundane chores only adds to that desperate feeling of uselessness. Shouldn&rsquo;t I be doing something exciting, grand, and life-altering right now instead of vacuuming and cleaning up the kids&rsquo; mess? Thinking like this often leaves me frustrated as I can&rsquo;t escape the current situation we are in.</p>
<p>So instead of approaching chores as a chore&mdash;even that word feels painful to pronounce&mdash;I try to approach them as a housekeeping task. There is no difference, except that airing, dusting, vacuuming, adjusting, tidying, and cleaning sound more positive than doing a <em>chore</em>. Let&rsquo;s celebrate mundaneness.</p>
<p>Housekeeping yields two things. First, the work in and around the house is done: you are indeed entitled to the sweat of your brow. Is that <em>BioShock</em> reference too obtuse? I think not. It&rsquo;s always a pleasure to pause and look around: things are clean! Revel in that moment because the dog is about to shake his tail spreading more gunk and the kids are about to storm up to these boxes of neatly stored toys to empty them after than you can blink. Is that one of the cats I hear making puke noises again?</p>
<p>Second, doing the work invites a few mindful moments<sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup> where your undivided attention to these tasks might help to calm your mind, leaving you with less instead of more frustration. Remember, life is mundane. You might feel upset reading that sentence, claiming I generalised and your life is special. Fine: my life is currently mundane. Does that make you feel better? How to deal with mundaneness is one of life&rsquo;s greatest challenges. Simply enjoying both the process and the result of housekeeping well get you a long way.</p>
<p>Why does mundane sound so negative? Its meaning, according to various dictionaries, is <em>belonging to or characteristic of the earth</em>&mdash;as opposed to spiritual things. Biking to work is mundane. Eating is mundane. Reading a book is mundane. Typing these words in a text editor to upload them onto this website is mundane. All of these can also be called <em>chores</em> if the interpretation is <em>a routine task or job</em>.</p>
<p>When looking for other blog posts on housekeeping, I can only find <em>technical</em> blog housekeeping announcements: small tweaks made to the theme, the engine, the publication process. Tiny fixes for the RSS feed, a slight repositioning of the header image. Doing all these things&mdash;also known as fiddling with the blog&mdash;is fun and yields a more pleasant blog. Of course, the big difference between that housekeeping and real housekeeping is that real housekeeping can overwhelm you (especially if you have two young kids) and real housekeeping keeps on coming back again and again in exactly the same form. Once that header image on your blog has been repositioned, it&rsquo;s not likely to fall down again, but the kitchen floor will need vacuuming again and again.</p>
<p>The biggest difference between virtual &ldquo;blog&rdquo; housekeeping and real &ldquo;house&rdquo; housekeeping is that you <em>share</em> that house and expect housemates to take part in the cleaning. The young kids just don&rsquo;t because they can&rsquo;t. Also, my interpretation of housekeeping isn&rsquo;t the same as my wife&rsquo;s. She often accuses me of Monica-behaviour (and I don&rsquo;t think I need to explain this one). This is often the cause of squabbles that undermine my entire point, but still.</p>
<p>Don&rsquo;t forget to dust the duster.</p>
